<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laurentian Codex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1377</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Laurentian Chronicle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Laurentian Codex is a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copied in 1377 by the monk Lavrenty for Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-of-suzdal">Dmitry Konstantinovich of Suzdal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>. It contains the oldest surviving copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, followed by a continuation based on the chronicles of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> to 1305. It is held in the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in St Petersburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laurentian-codex.jpg" alt="Laurentian Codex" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolay Omonov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurentian_Codex_07,_Calling_of_varangians.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
